KW  - orbit determination
KW  - secondary payload
PB  - John Wiley & Sons, Ltd
UR  - https://doi.org/10.1029/2025EA004740
Y2  - 2026/09/04
N2  - Abstract Lunar Trailblazer is a smallsat mission launched on 27 February 2025 as a secondary payload with the Intuitive Machines IM-2 mission. After insertion into a near-polar circular orbit around the Moon, the mission's goal was to search and characterize lunar water, primarily looking for ice in permanently shadowed craters and water bound in minerals. As a rideshare, a major challenge involved designing a feasible and robust reference trajectory from launch conditions (date and injection states) that could not be controlled. This paper describes the innovative, systematic trajectory and maneuver design process used to develop a robust reference trajectory that satisfies all requirements. The February 27 reference trajectory is described in detail to demonstrate this process. Early launch operations are also discussed, including the unfortunate loss of signal and the unconventional use of radar and optical astrometry for spacecraft orbit determination. Finally, lessons learned from a mission design and navigation (MDNav) perspective are presented to inform the design of similar future smallsat missions.
ER  - 

TY  - JOUR
T1  - Tailoring Model-Based Systems Engineering for Sub-100 g PlanarSats: A Power-First View Stack
AU  - Uludaǧ, Mehmet Şevket
